drumee-schemas (2.7.5) stable; urgency=medium

  * setup-schemas: createHub matches the yp.entity row from desk_create_hub instead of the
    MFS node row that the nested mfs_trash_init leaks into the same result. In that node row
    actual_home_id is NULL (the trash node's home is itself), so the old condition
    `r.vhost && r.actual_home_id` never held, createHub returned undefined, and createAdmin
    died three frames later on `wp.id` with "Cannot read properties of undefined". It read
    like a database fault because the log printed "Failed to create hub" beside a result set
    showing failed:0, a db_name and an mfs_root — everything HAD worked except this loop.
    It now also throws rather than returning undefined: every caller uses the result
    immediately as wp.id, so an undefined return only moved the failure somewhere that could
    not explain it.
  * This is what blocked provisioning on the container channel. Verified end to end: five
    databases, the entity pool, all four system accounts, the admin, the RSA keypair and the
    welcome file, and the stack then serves HTTP 301 / HTTPS 200 with the real page.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 16:40:41 +0200

drumee-schemas (2.7.4) stable; urgency=medium

  * setup-schemas connects where db.json says. Sixteen mariadb invocations in
    lib/utils.js and lib/schema.js were a bare `mariadb`, which uses the local unix
    socket — right for a native install and impossible when the database is a container
    of its own. populate.js died with "Can't connect to local server through socket
    '/run/mysqld/mysqld.sock'" on a stack whose db.json said host=mariadb, and no
    configuration could have helped because the host never reached the client.
    .
    dbConnFlags() returns "" for a local database, which is every native install, so
    those commands stay byte-identical and keep authenticating by unix_socket. It
    reports a malformed db.json rather than silently falling back to the socket.
    .
    This is what lets ONE populate serve both channels instead of the container fork
    that existed only because of this assumption.

 -- somanos.sar@drumee.com  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 11:11:50 +0200

drumee-schemas (2.7.3) stable; urgency=medium

  * Deliver the payload and touch no database when DRUMEE_BUILD_TIME is set — the same
    flag and the same reason as drumee-infra (§5). bin/install restores a mariabackup
    PHYSICAL snapshot into /var/lib/mysql with --copy-back, creates the system accounts
    and sends a welcome email; none of that can happen in a `docker build`, and it did
    not fail quietly. It stopped a MariaDB that was never running, could not find
    /srv/db or /var/log/drumee, and ended with "FATAL: seed restore (mariabackup
    --copy-back) failed", leaving drumee-role-schemas unconfigured and failing the whole
    image build. Measured.
    .
    The container channel bootstraps logically instead — the base databases from the
    schemas repo's own templates/factory/ tree, over TCP, from the schemas role's
    entrypoint — because a physical restore cannot reach a database that lives in
    another container.

 -- somanos.sar@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 17:02:56 +0200

drumee-schemas (2.7.2) stable; urgency=medium

  * Build from schemas' regenerated host-neutral templates. 2.7.1 shipped a
    neutral seed, but one built from the *previous* template generation: the
    triggers in it were still the corrupt DDL the old greedy DEFINER sed produced,
    so all three yp disk_usage triggers arrived only because schemas-init replays
    the patch corpus over them. The regenerated templates carry intact
    `CREATE ... TRIGGER `name`` headers, so the seed now supplies them directly
    and that replay stops being load-bearing.
    .
    Also fixes 98 surviving `DEFINER=``@`localhost`` clauses — an empty definer
    user, which the first strip_definer pattern did not match because it required
    at least one character. Restoring those on a host with no anonymous
    ''@'localhost' account is the exact failure that stripping DEFINER prevents.
    .
    schemas/build.sh is pinned to the fix/neutral-templates branch until it merges
    into preview.
    .
    Seed rebuilt offline from the branch: loads with zero errors (2.7.1's
    templates produced three ERROR 1064), and the restored snapshot holds 3 yp
    triggers, 762 routines, 144 tables, and 0 rows in yp.entity, trash.entity and
    mailserver.domains.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 09:09:31 +0200

drumee-schemas (2.7.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Ship a host-neutral seed. seeds.tgz was built by running container-populate.js
    inside the seed builder, which stocked the factory pool and created the fixed
    system accounts — so the seed described that container rather than a Drumee.
    Entities carry an ABSOLUTE home_dir, built from the builder's
    DRUMEE_DATA_DIR=/data, and a native install allocates users straight out of
    that pool. Measured on a from-scratch box: 20 of 115 entities sat under
    /data/mfs, including the admin account and the Portal and System hubs, while
    nginx serves `alias /srv/data/mfs/`. Their files were unreachable.
    .
    Separately, `nobody` is the one account with a fixed id (ID_NOBODY,
    'ffffffffffffffff'). The seed already held that row, so every install's
    createNobody -> updateEntries hit ER_DUP_ENTRY on all six of its UPDATEs;
    nobody kept the seed's dead /data/mfs home_dir and the pool entity drawn for
    it was stranded with its own database. guest and system don't force an id, so
    rather than failing they silently duplicated: guest+guest1, system+system1.
    .
    The seed now carries zero entities. setup-schemas' populate.js:stockFactory
    stocks the pool on the target host — idempotent, to POOL_COUNT — and this
    package's postinst runs bin/install before it counts the pool, so the
    EMPTY_FACTORY guard is satisfied. Stocking on the target is also the only way
    to get correct paths: only the target knows its own data_dir.
    .
    Asserted, not assumed: the seed builder refuses to publish a seed containing
    yp.entity rows, baked home_dir values, or a trash.entity left over from the
    template host, and schemas' bin/make-templates asserts the same for the
    templates feeding it.

 -- Somanos Sar <somanos.sar@drumee.com>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 16:38:42 +0200

drumee-schemas (2.7.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Upgrade the MariaDB system tables to the running server version before
    populate runs, instead of letting Debian do it concurrently. The seed carries
    the system tables of whatever server produced it, so a restore onto a
    different version has mariadb-upgrade rewriting mysql.proc while populate is
    loading routines into it. The two deadlock on that table, and because
    lock_wait_timeout defaults to 86400 the install does not fail — it hangs for a
    day, indistinguishable from a slow restore.
  * Stop fetching initial content over the network during populate. The wallpaper
    and tutorial import reached a host that no longer resolves, and the failure
    path raised a ReferenceError instead of being skipped, aborting database
    initialization and leaving the package half-configured.
  * Fix the factory-pool guard, which counted system accounts through a column
    that has never existed. The query always errored, the error was swallowed into
    a zero, and the warning therefore fired on every install — including healthy
    ones whose pool count in the same message contradicted it.

